---
import Legal from '../layouts/Legal.astro';
import legal from '../data/legal.json';
---
<Legal
  title="Terms of Service"
  updated="18 August 2026"
  description="The terms under which datadir provides the poddle service."
>
  <p class="lead">These terms govern your use of poddle. By creating an account or using the service, you agree to them.</p>

  <p>
    {legal.productName} is provided by <strong>{legal.entity}</strong> ("datadir", "we", "us"), a
    {legal.legalForm} with its registered seat at {legal.registeredOffice} (IČO {legal.ico}),
    registered in the {legal.registration}. "You" means the individual or organisation using the
    service; if you use it for an organisation, you confirm you are authorised to bind it.
  </p>

  <h2>The service</h2>
  <p>
    poddle is a credential broker and control plane for coding-agent sandboxes ("pods"). We provide the
    broker, identities, connectors, governance and audit, and - optionally - managed pod compute. You
    are responsible for the pods you run and the workloads inside them.
  </p>

  <h2>Accounts</h2>
  <p>
    You must provide accurate information, keep your credentials secure, and are responsible for all
    activity under your account. You must be at least 16 and able to form a binding contract to use
    poddle. Tell us promptly at <a href={`mailto:${legal.emails.security}`}>{legal.emails.security}</a>
    if you suspect unauthorised use.
  </p>

  <h2>Acceptable use</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Don't break the law, infringe others' rights, or violate a provider's or connector's terms.</li>
    <li>Don't attempt to defeat the isolation, governance, or security of the service or other tenants.</li>
    <li>Don't use the service to build a competing product, or resell or misrepresent it.</li>
    <li>Don't run workloads that harm the service or third parties (malware, spam, unlawful scraping, or the like).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your content &amp; infrastructure</h2>
  <p>
    You own your code, prompts, and data. When you connect a provider or a connector, you authorise
    poddle's broker to inject the relevant credential on your behalf on outbound requests. You are
    responsible for having the rights to use those providers and for complying with their terms. Where
    you bring your own compute, your relationship with that infrastructure provider is yours.
  </p>

  <h2>Third-party providers &amp; connectors</h2>
  <p>
    poddle brokers access to third-party services (model providers, forges, pipelines, clouds,
    databases). Those services are governed by their own terms and are not operated by us; we are not
    responsible for their availability, content, or acts. Your use of them through poddle does not
    change your agreement with them.
  </p>

  <h2>Fees &amp; billing</h2>
  <p>
    Paid plans are billed per developer as described on our <a href="/pricing">pricing page</a>. Managed
    pod compute, if used, is metered and billed in arrears. Fees are exclusive of taxes, which we add
    where required. Except where the law requires otherwise, fees are non-refundable. We may change
    pricing with at least 30 days' notice, effective at your next renewal.
  </p>

  <h2>Trials, beta &amp; early access</h2>
  <p>
    Trials and features labelled beta, preview, or early access are provided "as is", may change or be
    withdrawn at any time, and may have limits or defects. Don't rely on them for production-critical
    work.
  </p>

  <h2>Feedback</h2>
  <p>
    If you send us ideas or suggestions, you grant us a perpetual, worldwide, royalty-free licence to
    use them to improve the service, with no obligation to you.
  </p>

  <h2>Service availability &amp; support</h2>
  <p>
    We work to keep poddle available and provide support appropriate to your plan, but we do not
    guarantee uninterrupted service unless a separate service-level agreement says so. We may perform
    maintenance and will give reasonable notice of planned downtime where practical.
  </p>

  <h2>Confidentiality</h2>
  <p>
    Each party may receive non-public information from the other. The recipient will protect it with
    reasonable care and use it only to provide or use the service, except where disclosure is required
    by law. This does not apply to information that is or becomes public through no fault of the
